{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And they came.</b> The Gaon [Saadia] said that there were seventy types of date palms, and others added: for each and every tribe, and others said: for each and every man. And there is no need for these words, for the springs, which were twelve, and the seventy date palms were not created now for the sake of Israel, and this is not the season of ripening dates. And in my opinion, Israel did not stay in Marah except one day, and they lingered in Elim about twenty days. Therefore Scripture says, \"and they encamped there by the waters.\" Therefore it says in the verse that comes after it, \"for on the fifteenth day of the second month they set out from Elim\" (Exodus 16:1) — and behold, in fifteen days they encamped in four places, and these are: the wilderness of Sin, and Dophkah, and Alush, and Rephidim, for at the beginning of the third month they came to the wilderness of Sinai. And he mentioned the date palms to inform that the waters are sweet, the opposite of the waters of Marah, for date palms do not thrive in a land whose waters are bitter."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:57.647643997Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}